{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be your doctor during this stay. You came in with nausea, vomiting, and high blood sugar. Can you tell me what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:08:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I haven’t been able to keep anything down since last night. I started feeling anxious and then the nausea hit. I vomited a few times—watery and brown, no blood. I knew my sugars would be high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:09:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds like your usual gastroparesis flare. We checked your blood sugar in the ER—it was in the 300s—and your anion gap was elevated, so you were in mild DKA. We gave you insulin and fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:10:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. I was worried it was something worse. Is that why my blood pressure was so high?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your systolic was up to 210. That often happens with nausea and vomiting—it’s a Valsalva effect. We gave you labetalol to bring it down and will restart your lisinopril once you can take pills."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:13:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had this happen before. It’s scary when my BP spikes like that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:14:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re monitoring it closely. For now, you’ll stay NPO—no food or drink by mouth—so your stomach can rest. We’re giving you IV fluids and insulin through a continuous drip."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:15:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "How long will I be on the IV insulin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Until your sugars stabilize and you can eat again. Once you start tolerating food, we’ll switch back to your usual insulin—glargine and a sliding scale."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:18:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’ve been on that for years, but sometimes it’s hard to control when I’m sick."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:19:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. That’s why we’re being aggressive. We also gave you Zofran and Compazine for nausea, and Ativan for anxiety. You were given Ativan in the ER, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:20:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I remember that. It helped calm me down. I’ve been on citalopram for depression, but I think the anxiety triggers the vomiting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s common. We’ve consulted psychiatry. They recommend continuing your citalopram and using Ativan as needed for anxiety. We’ll start you on oral Ativan once you can swallow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:23:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I ran out of Ativan before coming in. Is that okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:24:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll give you a prescription when you leave. Just don’t take too much—especially not before driving. We want it to help, not cause drowsiness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2199-04-16 15:25:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. I’ll be careful."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2199-04-17 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs from last night look better. Anion gap is normal now—13—and glucose is 147. Blood cultures and urine are negative, so no infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2199-04-17 08:16:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. Can I try drinking something?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2199-04-17 08:17:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll start with small sips of water. If you keep that down, we’ll advance to clear liquids. We also did a chest X-ray yesterday—no pneumonia or other issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2199-04-17 08:18:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I saw the tube in my chest on the X-ray. Is that my port?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2199-04-17 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, the catheter tip is in your SVC. It’s working well for access. The X-ray also showed your heart is a bit enlarged, but no fluid or infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2199-04-17 08:21:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had that before. Is it from the high blood pressure?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2199-04-17 08:22:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. Your BP has been better—around 150s systolic—since we restarted lisinopril. We’ll keep monitoring it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2199-04-17 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well with clear liquids. How’s your nausea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2199-04-17 14:31:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. I haven’t vomited since yesterday afternoon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2199-04-17 14:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. We’re going to try a small meal—soft foods like toast and applesauce. If you tolerate that, we’ll keep advancing your diet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2199-04-17 14:33:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m nervous it might come back up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2199-04-17 14:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll go slow. And we’ll keep the anti-nausea meds going—Compazine and Zofran. You’re also on metoclopramide now, which helps your stomach empty."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2199-04-17 14:36:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I used to take that three times a day. The note says four times?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2199-04-17 14:37:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re increasing it to four times a day—before meals and at bedtime. It might help prevent future flares."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2199-04-18 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You slept well and kept down dinner. That’s a big step. We’re going to switch you from the insulin drip to your glargine at night and a sliding scale during the day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2199-04-18 09:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My sugars were high earlier this morning though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2199-04-18 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, they were in the 300s. We adjusted your sliding scale and started you on 28 units of glargine at bedtime. That should help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2199-04-18 09:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve needed higher doses before. Diabetes is harder to control when I’m stressed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2199-04-18 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll work with your diabetes team after discharge to fine-tune it. For now, you’re doing well. Psychiatry also cleared you—they say no psychiatric issues prevent discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2199-04-18 09:06:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad. I’ll call the psychiatrist my sister sees. I need to get back into therapy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2199-04-19 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs are stable. Hemoglobin is a bit low—11.4—but that’s been chronic. We’re not concerned about bleeding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2199-04-19 10:16:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had iron deficiency for years. Should I take supplements?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2199-04-19 10:17:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Talk to your PCP about that. We’re not starting anything now, but it’s good to follow up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2199-04-19 10:18:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I have a PCP appointment next week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve been eating regular meals and your sugars are staying below 200. That’s excellent progress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel so much better. I can’t believe how fast this came on."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Stress and anxiety can trigger these episodes. That’s why we’re emphasizing using Ativan early when you feel anxious or nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do that. I’ll also try to avoid situations that make me anxious."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That helps, but it’s not always possible. The key is catching it early. We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:06:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What meds am I going home on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Same as before: lisinopril, citalopram, metoclopramide four times a day, and your insulin. We’re giving you a two-week supply of Ativan to have on hand."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:08:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No amitriptyline?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2199-04-20 11:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not this time. We focused on the acute issues. Talk to your doctor about restarting it if you need help sleeping."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2199-04-21 09:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re cleared to go home. Your vitals are stable—BP 158/88, heart rate 83. You’re alert and walking fine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2199-04-21 09:31:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2199-04-21 09:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Here’s your discharge summary. Take Ativan if you feel anxious or nauseous. Follow up with your diabetes doctor and PCP. We also referred you to social work for support."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2199-04-21 09:33:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll make those appointments. I really appreciate the help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2199-04-21 09:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. And remember—don’t drive on Ativan. Call us if you can’t keep fluids down or your sugars go above 300."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2199-04-21 09:36:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks again, Dr. Lee."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2199-04-16 15:07:00",
    "admission_end": "2199-04-21 16:38:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with type 1 diabetes, gastroparesis, and a history of anxiety and depression was admitted for nausea, vomiting, and diabetic ketoacidosis. She presented with elevated blood glucose, an anion gap of 21, and hypertensive urgency with systolic blood pressure up to 210, which resolved with treatment. She was managed with an insulin drip, IV fluids, and antiemetics, and transitioned to subcutaneous insulin as her condition improved. Anxiety was addressed with Ativan and continuation of citalopram, with psychiatry support. Her diet was advanced as nausea resolved, and she stabilized over five days. She was discharged home with instructions to use Ativan for early anxiety or nausea, continue her medications including increased metoclopramide, and follow up with her PCP, diabetes team, and a psychiatrist. A social work referral was made for ongoing support.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Hyposmolality and/or hyponatremia",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Diabetes with ketoacidosis, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Persistent vomiting",
      "Polyneuropathy in diabetes",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Esophagitis, unspecified",
      "Iron deficiency anemia, unspecified",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified"
    ]
  }
}
